WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.250
- Hey, legal eagles, you hear a lot

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.410
about case briefs in law school.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.850
What is a case brief?
Why do people do them?

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:10.560
And why do students
hate them so damn much?

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.460
What can you do to get the benefits

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.230
without the hassle of case briefs?

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:17.500
Well, today we're answering
all of those questions,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:18.360
and by the end of the video,

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:20.700
you'll know exactly what
goes into a case brief

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.420
and how to supercharge them.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:23.273
So stick around.

00:00:24.305 --> 00:00:26.888
(upbeat music)

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.890
Before we get into the case brief,

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:33.820
we need to talk about law
school in general for a second.

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:36.640
Law school is taught
using the Case Method,

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.070
or the inductive approach
of learning the law

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:40.520
through cases that have already,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.470
supposedly, applied the law.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.313
So for the sake of class discussion,

00:00:44.313 --> 00:00:47.140
professors assign
several cases for reading

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.180
before students get to class.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:52.240
That can result in having to
read dozens of cases a night

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.910
and potentially hundreds
of pages worth of cases.

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:57.320
So let's start with what is a case

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.000
in the law school context?

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.460
I know this might seem simplistic,

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:02.450
but stick with me just for a second.

00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:04.050
In law school, when you read a case,

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.090
what you're really reading
is an appellate opinion.

00:01:07.090 --> 00:01:10.210
That is, after the trial or
judgment in the court below,

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:12.870
one of the parties claims
that the trial court was wrong

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.800
and appeals the decision
to the court of appeals.

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:17.510
The factual record is set.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.850
You can't change it, you can't
call additional witnesses,

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.260
there's no additional evidence.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:23.530
The parties write legal briefs

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:25.960
and after weighing the
briefs of both sides,

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.310
the appellate court will
then issue a written opinion.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:32.740
Usually that's on a narrow issue of law.

00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:35.060
In theory, the appellate court summaries

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.700
the important facts of the case,
but not necessarily always,

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:41.910
and in theory, they write
only as much analysis

00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.000
as is necessary to adjudicate the appeal.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.010
But this is almost never the case.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.040
The appellate opinions
are almost always longer

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.650
than they need to be,
sometimes to provide context

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.850
and sometimes because judges just

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:56.960
like to hear themselves talk.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:01:59.280
So, in law school, a case brief

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.630
is a summary of a particular
appellate opinion.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:04.900
Standard advice says that your

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.800
case brief should look like this.

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:08.420
It should start with the case citation.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.450
You should have the name of both parties,

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.900
which is usually something
like Jones versus Smith.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.870
It should include the publisher
of the particular case,

00:02:16.870 --> 00:02:19.080
the reporter that the
case can be found in.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.580
The court that issued the decision,

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.180
the year that the opinion was published.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:25.660
The procedural posture of the case,

00:02:25.660 --> 00:02:27.880
meaning how it got to the court of appeal

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:32.060
the history of the case, a
discussion of where it came from.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.670
The Statement of Facts, the
narrative story of the case.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.310
The issues that were decided on appeal.

00:02:38.310 --> 00:02:40.500
The arguments on either side.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.030
The policy implications
of the different ways

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.820
that the court of appeal could have gone.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.200
The rule of law or the black letter law

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.200
that can be inductively discerned
from the appellate court.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.950
The rationale that the appellate court

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:57.080
used to decide the case.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:59.900
And any particular dissents
or concurring opinions

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:02.790
that are not necessarily
the law of the case,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:04.660
but at least go with it.

00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:09.024
So a case brief, that's the
notes that you are taking

00:03:09.024 --> 00:03:11.100
regarding the particular case,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.190
regarding those details of the case.

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.370
It's a summary of the so called
important parts of the case.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.310
And by the way, as a free bonus

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.510
you can download our
"Guide to Case Briefs"

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:26.690
and learn how to do them the
right way in the link below.

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:29.740
But, why do students do these summaries?

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.777
Well, there are three primary reasons.

00:03:31.777 --> 00:03:34.660
The first is to learn how to read a case.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.830
Standard thinking says that
the more cases you read

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:40.310
the more you will be able
to think like a lawyer.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:42.370
And it's certainly true that in practice

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.350
you'll be reading lots and lots cases.

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.520
So it's a good idea to
understand what goes into a case,

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.910
why judges decide the way that they do,

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.564
and to be able to use
cases in your practice

00:03:54.564 --> 00:03:56.710
as both a sword and shield

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:00.250
when you're litigating a particular idea.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:04.340
The second reason is that you
need to prepare for finals.

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:08.513
So often you'll use cases on a final exam.

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.010
Obviously, most of what you
discuss in class is going to be

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:15.310
about the particular
cases that you're reading

00:04:15.310 --> 00:04:18.160
because that's the case method itself.

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:20.230
And so it's not surprising
that you'll use a lot

00:04:20.230 --> 00:04:22.170
of this information on a final exam.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.470
And when your talking about
an issue spotter exam,

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.190
as you'll often get in law school,

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:29.077
then you can often use
the cases that you discuss

00:04:29.077 --> 00:04:34.040
as analogies, as a way that the "court"

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:36.400
when it's your professor, should come out.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.410
And if you don't take notes
on the cases that you read

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:42.420
and the discussion that you
have about the particular cases,

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:44.310
by the time that finals comes around

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.440
you'll probably have
forgotten all of your cases.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.650
So it's good to have a written record

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:50.910
of what it was that you actually read.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.770
And of course, there's the third reason

00:04:52.770 --> 00:04:54.430
which is to prepare for class.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.500
And frankly, if everyone is being honest

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:58.570
this is probably the number one reason.

00:04:58.570 --> 00:04:59.940
You're compiling your notes so

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.970
that if the professor
calls on you, in class,

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:04.540
you're able to answer their questions

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.140
about the particular case.

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:10.000
This is the infamous
cold call in law school.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.360
And it is the bane of most
law students' existence.

00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:16.070
It's the Socratic Method
to pepper your students

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:17.710
with as many questions as possible

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.109
until they're simply unable to answer

00:05:20.109 --> 00:05:22.570
any more questions about a given subject.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:24.070
And the idea is that the more

00:05:24.070 --> 00:05:25.750
you're questioned about something

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:28.720
the more you will learn what's
important and what's not.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:31.340
And by the way, the funny thing is

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:34.678
that when class participation
doesn't count for anything

00:05:34.678 --> 00:05:38.579
spedning all of this time
on preparing for class

00:05:38.579 --> 00:05:42.870
instead of preparing for
finals can be a bad strategy.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:44.370
But I get it, it's terrifying

00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:46.750
to be called on by the professor in class,

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:50.490
so students are going to
err on the side of reading

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:53.820
their cases more carefully
than they might need to

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.350
and taking more copious
notes than they might need to

00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:00.560
in order to have a
fruitful class discussion.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.520
But here at Legal Eagle we think

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.020
that most case briefing that students do

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:07.220
is unnecessary and a waste of time.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:08.220
Why?

00:06:08.220 --> 00:06:10.250
Well, because you're going to be tested

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:13.020
using an issue spotting hypothetical.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:17.070
And we have an entire video
on what an issue spotting hypo

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:18.900
or exam essay looks like.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.534
You can check the link below.

00:06:20.534 --> 00:06:22.630
But on an issue spotter,

00:06:22.630 --> 00:06:25.550
you can't use 95% of the information

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:27.240
that you put in your case brief.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.623
It's a waste of time.

00:06:28.623 --> 00:06:29.456
Sure the professor might ask about

00:06:29.456 --> 00:06:33.220
all of that information in class,

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:34.760
but the professor can't ask

00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:37.260
that kind of information on the exam

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:39.840
and the test is what matters.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.160
And I can't emphasize that enough.

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:44.560
I've seen hundreds of
issue spotting answers

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.860
and never once did they give points

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.410
for the procedural posture
of a particular case

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:50.650
or the history of the case

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.390
or even the rational or the case name.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:56.560
An issue spotting exam, makes you pretend

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:59.590
that you're an attorney
and as in real life

00:06:59.590 --> 00:07:02.810
you're rarely using that extra
information about a case.

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:06.120
What you are using is the
narrative story of the case

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.860
or the black letter law
that you'll find in a case.

00:07:09.860 --> 00:07:13.820
You might apply just the
bare essential meaning

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:17.330
of what the law is, but ignore
all the rest of the story

00:07:17.330 --> 00:07:20.757
or you might use the
facts of a particular case

00:07:20.757 --> 00:07:23.690
to make an analogy to the issue,

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:27.110
or the hypo that's in the issue spotter

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:28.850
to make an argument one way or the other.

00:07:28.850 --> 00:07:31.300
To say that this case is like the case

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.790
that you read before in
that the facts are similar

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.990
or that it should come out differently

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:37.633
because the facts are different.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:42.148
But the thing is, the case
brief takes up so much time.

00:07:42.148 --> 00:07:44.200
It takes up hundreds of hours

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:47.100
and you're focusing on
generally the wrong thing.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:49.090
We think that instead of doing

00:07:49.090 --> 00:07:52.100
the standard style of case briefing,

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.420
where you highlight your case brief

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:55.670
and make it look like a rainbow

00:07:55.670 --> 00:07:57.740
with all the different
colors of the highlighter,

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.540
and write all of those different,

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:02.230
dozen things that you can write

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:04.560
about a particular case
in your case brief,

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.450
we think what you should do

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:07.960
what we call a three point brief.

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:11.344
A three point brief focuses
only on the three things

00:08:11.344 --> 00:08:13.400
that your professor cares about.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.820
And more importantly it's
the only three things

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:18.960
that you're actually going
to use on your final exam.

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:20.920
You can learn how to
write a three point brief

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.810
in our free "Guide to
Law School Case Briefs."

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:24.920
And the link to that is down below.

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.420
It will, I promise, save you hundreds,

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:31.348
if not thousands of hours
during your law school career.

00:08:31.348 --> 00:08:33.417
And also check out our video on

